# The EM algorithm {#sec-em}

Recall @thm-consistency from @sec-mle: maximum likelihood is worth chasing. But when
each observation hides a latent label, the likelihood is a sum inside a logarithm and
has no closed-form maximizer. Expectation maximization sidesteps this by optimizing a
lower bound instead, one whose maximizer we can write down.

::: {.theorem #thm-elbo}
For any distribution $q$ over the latent variables,
$$ \log p(x) \;\ge\; \mathbb{E}_{q}\!\left[\log \frac{p(x, z)}{q(z)}\right] \;=:\; \mathcal{L}(q, \theta). $$
The gap is exactly $\mathrm{KL}\!\left(q \,\|\, p(z \mid x)\right)$, so the bound is tight
when $q$ is the posterior.
:::

::: {.proof collapse="true"}
Start from $\log p(x) = \mathbb{E}_q[\log p(x)]$ and substitute
$p(x) = p(x, z) / p(z \mid x)$. Adding and subtracting $\log q(z)$ inside the expectation
splits the logarithm into $\mathcal{L}(q, \theta)$ plus
$\mathrm{KL}(q \,\|\, p(z \mid x))$, and the divergence is non-negative by Gibbs'
inequality.
:::

The E-step sets $q$ to the current posterior over the latents; every responsibility is a
posterior mean in the sense of @def-expectation. The M-step then maximizes the bound in
closed form. Here is that update, walked through line by line:

::: {.code-walkthrough}
```python
def m_step(x, resp):
    w = resp.sum(axis=0)
    mu = (resp.T @ x) / w[:, None]
    return mu, w / w.sum()
```

::: {.step lines="2"}
Sum the responsibilities down the columns to get each component's **effective count**,
the soft number of points it currently owns.
:::

::: {.step lines="3"}
Re-estimate each component **mean** as a responsibility-weighted average of the data,
so points a component is confident about pull its mean hardest.
:::

::: {.step lines="4"}
The new **mixing weights** are just the normalized counts, and the updated means are
returned for the next E-step.
:::
:::

A quick numerical sanity check that the counts sum to the number of points:

```{python}
#| echo: true
import numpy as np
resp = np.array([[0.9, 0.1], [0.2, 0.8], [0.6, 0.4]])
print("effective counts:", resp.sum(axis=0), "-> total", resp.sum())
```

By @thm-elbo each round can only raise the bound, so EM climbs toward a local optimum of
the very likelihood @thm-consistency told us to trust.
